  "text": "A foreign mother argued with Jesus and won. Matthew fifteen. Jesus goes into the coasts of Tyre and Sidon, and a woman of Canaan comes out crying after him. Have mercy on me, O Lord, thou son of David, my daughter is grievously vexed with a devil. And he answered her not a word. The disciples want her sent away because she keeps crying after them. Then he says he was sent to the lost sheep of the house of Israel. She comes and worships him and says three words. Lord, help me. And he gives her the hard line. It is not meet to take the children's bread, and to cast it to dogs. Now, people argue about the tone of that sentence, and the text does not give us tone, so hold your theory loosely. What is not in dispute is her answer. Truth, Lord, yet the dogs eat of the crumbs which fall from their masters' table. She takes his metaphor, accepts the terms, and turns it. And he says, O woman, great is thy faith. Be it unto thee even as thou wilt. And her daughter was made whole from that very hour.",
